# Passive voice makes the thing acted on the subject of the sentence. It
# names the actor, which grammar calls the agent, after `by` or not at
# all (`the file is read by the parser`, `the job is retried`). The rule
# matches a form of `be` followed by a participle, the `-ing` or `-ed`
# form of a verb (`retried`, `written`).
#
# The Recommended tier reports a passive after a modal verb, a helping
# verb that marks possibility, ability, or obligation, such as `can`,
# `might`, and `must` (`the timeout can be configured`). The
# future-tense rule reports the `will` in `will be` plus a participle.
# This tier reports every other passive. It is a suggestion, because
# naming the actor there helps less often. The bare word `be` counts
# only when the token before it is not a modal verb, `not`, or an adverb
# (`can also be configured`). The two tiers therefore never report the
# same span. Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes that token
# and narrows the finding back to the passive. A passive after `to`
# stays in this tier (`to be configured`).
#
# Forms of `get` are left out on purpose. Matching is case-insensitive,
# so a `get VERB` branch fires on `Get returns the value` in a Go doc
# comment. Harper's tagger also rarely marks the participle after `gets`
# (`gets flushed` goes unflagged either way).
#
# The filter `<VERB, !@stative>` requires one token to be a verb and not
# one of the stative words. Stative words name a state rather than an
# action, and the tagger also marks them as verbs. The stative list holds a
# progressive, a form of `be` plus an `-ing` verb (`has been running` is
# active voice). It also holds the participles that docs use as
# adjectives (`the flag is set`, `is enabled by default`, `the port is
# stuck`) and the naming idioms (`is called name`, `is known as a
# handle`, `is meant for testing`). The purpose wrappers the intent rule
# reports sit there too (`is designed to`, `is intended to`), so each
# draws one finding. `recommended`, `advised`, and `encouraged` are
# stative for the same reason, because the obligation rule reports `is
# recommended` and `are encouraged to`.
#
# The `by` branch exists because the tagger misses many participles (`is
# guarded by a lock`). It accepts any word the tagger does not mark as
# an adjective, adverb, noun, or preposition, so a degree (`off by one`)
# and a deadline (`empty by the time`) pass. Weir has no token class for
# a number, so the object of `by` must be a pronoun, a proper noun, or a
# determiner instead. A determiner is a word that introduces a noun,
# such as `the`, `a`, or `this`. That skips a dimension (`64 by 64`), a
# criterion (`keyed by path`), and the phrases where `by` names no actor
# (`by default`, `by design`, `by hand`). `by a lock`, `by the
# scheduler`, and `by git` still count as agents.
#
# The tagger marks many participles as adjectives (`cached`,
# `retried`, `flushed`), so `missed` lists the common ones by hand.
# `exported` and `deprecated` stay out, because a Go doc uses each to
# name a state (`the field is exported`, `the method is deprecated`).
# `not` sits beside the adverb slot, because the tagger does not mark it
# as one (`the value is not written`).
#
# The `!-` glued after the verb skips a hyphenated compound whose
# first half the tagger marks as a verb (`is read-only`, `are
# fail-open`). It matches at the end of a clause too, so `the file is
# skipped.` still counts.
#
# Stative words still read as a passive when an agent follows them
# (`the flag is set by the caller`, `the binary is built by the
# pipeline`), so `agentive` lists the stative words that take one.
# The agent must start with a determiner, pronoun, or proper noun, as
# in the `by` branch, and the determiner must not open `by the time`
# or `by the way`.
#
# `being` takes any participle but `said`, because `That being said` is
# the opener the concessive rule reports. The first branch takes every
# other form of `be` through the filter `<@be, !being>`.
#
# Matching ignores case, so a Go method named `Is` reads as the verb
# (`errors.Is matches when any error in the tree matches`). The forms
# of `be` count only when the token glued before them is not a period.
# Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes that token and then
# narrows the finding back to the passive. A sentence that opens with
# a form of `be` is usually a question. A list item that continues its
# lead-in also opens with one (`- are created automatically`), and the
# filter misses that passive. Four such items turned up in about
# 900 human-written docs, fewer than the method names a Go commit
# message mentions. `live` is stative because the tagger marks it as a
# verb (`a caller whose context is live`).
#
# Two forms stay flagged on purpose. One is an identifier used as a noun
# after `is` (`The default is print`), which belongs in a code span. The
# other is a participle that names the state an event leaves behind
# (`The issue is closed`). The tagger cannot tell it from the event, and
# the skill rewrites it anyway.
#
# It hit 6150 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled hits
# were passives.
expr stative [PROG, missing, done, set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, available, stuck, broken, gone, finished, lost, left, known, frozen, wedged, based, tied, built, made, fixed, live, called, named, meant, supposed, recommended, designed, intended, bound, referred, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned, encouraged]
expr missed [cached, compiled, computed, configured, decoded, discarded, encoded, evicted, fetched, flushed, hashed, linted, normalized, parsed, patched, persisted, propagated, queued, redirected, reset, restarted, retried, revoked, rotated, sanitized, serialized, stripped, synced, tagged, truncated, validated]
expr be [is, are, was, were, been, being, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't]
expr verb [<VERB, !@stative>, @missed]
expr agentive [set, unset, enabled, disabled, required, needed, expected, allowed, supported, broken, known, built, made, fixed, called, named, designed, intended, bound, prepared, advised, assured, reminded, warned]
expr agent [(DET ![time, way]), PRON, PROPN]
expr passive [(<@be, !being> [@verb, ([ADV, not] @verb)]!-), (@be <!@stative, !ADJ, !ADV, !NOUN, !ADP, !DET, !PRON, !INTJ> by [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (@be [@agentive, ([ADV, not] @agentive)] by @agent)]
expr modal [must, should, can, cannot, could, may, might, will, would, shall, can't, won't, shouldn't, couldn't, wouldn't, mustn't]
expr bare [(be [@verb, ([ADV, not] @verb)]!-), (be <!@stative, !ADJ, !ADV, !NOUN, !ADP, !DET, !PRON, !INTJ> by [DET, PRON, PROPN]), (be [@agentive, ([ADV, not] @agentive)] by @agent)]
expr main [<(!(.)@passive), @passive>, <(![@modal, not, ADV] @bare), @bare>, (being [<@verb, !said>, ([ADV, not] @verb)]!-)]
let message "Passive voice. Name the actor when the reader needs to know who acts."
let description "Flags a passive without a modal verb before it, such as `the job is retried` or `the map is guarded by a lock`."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The scheduler retries the job."
allows "The scheduler is retrying the job."
allows "The job has been running for an hour."
allows "She is tired."
allows "The lock is free."
allows "There is a lock."
allows "Get returns the value stored under key."
allows "The flag is enabled by default."
allows "When the flag is set, the hook skips the file."
allows "The file is missing."
allows "The count is off by one."
allows "The queue is empty by the time the loop runs."
allows "The image is 64 by 64 pixels."
allows "The two panes are side by side."
allows "The output is stable by design."
allows "The mapping is done by hand."
allows "The migration is done by Friday."
allows "The field is called name."
allows "The type is known as a handle."
allows "The flag is meant for testing."
allows "The hook is designed to run after every write."
allows "It is recommended that you pin the version."
allows "The field is exported."
allows "The method is deprecated."
allows "The port is stuck in CLOSE_WAIT."
allows "Be warned that the flag is sticky."
allows "The default is lookup by id."
allows "The answer is yes by the way."
allows "The flag is set by the time the hook runs."
allows "The value is set by hand."
allows "The lock is read-only while the sweep runs."
allows "Reads are fail-open and writes are fail-closed."
allows "That being said, the hook skips the file."
allows "The errors.Is call matches when any error in the tree matches."
allows "A caller whose context is live loads again."
allows "The timeout can be configured."
allows "The flag can also be configured."
allows "The lock must not be copied."
allows "Users are encouraged to pin the version."
